ay T3; Play B6; Play B5; Play T4; Play W6]\u3002", "reasoning": "", "output": "**Evaluation:**  \nMy hand currently has 8 tiles (W6, T3, T4, T5, T6, T7, B5, B6) plus two exposed melds: [B3,B4,B5] and [T2,T3,T4].  \nI need to form a winning hand of 4 melds + 1 pair, so I need to complete 1 more meld and a pair.  \n\nLooking at my hand tiles:  \n- **T3, T4, T5, T6, T7** are a strong Bamboo sequence cluster.  \n- **B5, B6** could pair with B5 from the meld [B3,B4,B5]? Actually no \u2014 melded tiles are locked, can't be used for new combinations except for patterns.  \n- **W6** is isolated.  \n\nI have a **Half Flush** possibility: all tiles are Bamboo (T) + one meld in Dots (B) + W6 as an outlier. If I discard W6, I'll have only Bamboo and Dots (plus honors? none in hand). But Half Flush requires only one suit + honors, so having two suits (Bamboo and Dots) won't qualify. So not a good path.  \n\nBetter: aim for **All Chows** or **Mixed Straight**?  \n\nFrom the unshown tiles:  \n- T5 (3 left), T6 (4 left), T7 (4 left) are relatively safe discards.  \n- T3 and T4 are part of my exposed meld [T2,T3,T4], so holding extra T3/T4 is less useful unless I go for Triple Chow patterns, but I already used T3,T4 in a meld.  \n\nDiscarding **W6** removes the only non-Bamboo/non-Dot tile, making my hand more uniform, but not fully one suit.  \n\nThe exposed melds:  \n1. [B3,B4,B5] \u2014 Dots chow  \n2. [T2,T3,T4] \u2014 Bamboo chow  \n\nSo if I keep Bamboo run T3\u2013T7, I can form more Bamboo chows:  \n- T3,T4,T5 chow (but T3,T4 already used in meld \u2014 can't reuse tiles for new melds)  \nWait \u2014 important: tiles in exposed melds are fixed, can't be rearranged with hand tiles. So T3 and T4 in hand are separate from T3,T4 in meld.  \n\nActually, my hand T3,T4,T5 could make a chow if I get T2 or T6? No, chow needs three consecutive: T3,T4,T5 is already a chow in hand \u2014 yes! That\u2019s a complete chow (T3,T4,T5).  \nSo in hand I have chow T3,T4,T5 plus extra T6,T7 for